# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# The 0xF6 health counters' own symbol, rsourced by the applications that have
# a host to answer - the same arrangement as Kconfig.antr_api and
# Kconfig.dongle_main beside it. apps/hrm_ble does not source it: a node has no
# serial host to request 0xF6 from, so the counters would have no way out.

config ANT_DONGLE_HEALTH_COUNTERS
	bool "Count what the dongle threw away, readable as MESG_RADIANT_HEALTH (0xF6)"
	help
	  Counts silent, dongle-side loss - USB TX queue drops, event-queue
	  overflows, RX backpressure, refused network keys, scheduler misses
	  and denials, burst back-pressure stalls, and the two halves of the
	  sweep's declined-to-post reason - and answers them to a host that
	  sends MESG_REQUEST (0x4D) for 0xF6. See apps/common/ant_health.h.

	  WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN THE COUNTERS THEMSELVES.
	  tools/ab_gates.toml's `unexplained_loss.max = 0` is a *sitting
	  invalidator*: a hole in the accumulator with no RX_FAIL means the
	  loss happened on the host or inside the dongle, and
	  loss_accounting() cannot tell those two apart. This splits that
	  bucket with a firmware-side witness. The check has already caught
	  0.4 pp of imaginary loss once.

	  It is also the only record that survives on the board most people
	  flash: a Feather has no debugger, so the LOG_WRN that reports a
	  dropped event today goes nowhere at all.

	  OFF BY DEFAULT, and the reason is mechanical rather than cautious.
	  The `zero-cost` CI job asserts identical section sizes and symbol
	  sets against the merge base, and counters on the common path fire
	  it. With this off every note() compiles to nothing - a macro, not an
	  empty function, so nothing evaluates its arguments either - and the
	  shipping image is byte-identical. That keeps `zero-cost` a real
	  check rather than one that had to be weakened to admit this.

	  Turning it on does NOT change what the dongle does, only what it can
	  be asked. Nothing is ever emitted unsolicited, so the conformance
	  transcripts tools/ab_gates.toml pins byte-for-byte are unaffected.

	  It is NOT a request to deepen a queue. See ANT_TX_QUEUE_DEPTH in
	  apps/common/usb_ant_class.c: at 3103 msg/s depth 32 is about 10 ms
	  of buffer, and a deeper one converts a drop into latency, which is
	  the wrong trade for a protocol whose value is a 0.345 ms p50.
